  "reason": "Complete construction and prerequisite audit; see research/phase-2-next-20-batch-4.notes.md and coverage.json. Source D, §4.2 pp.219–220; documented alternative-proof disposition, no raw fetch claim. The products are F_k-measurable by measurable arithmetic and the increasing filtration, and are L1 by the domain hypothesis. Consequently the finite sum is adapted and integrable. If |H_k|<=C_k<infinity a.s. with deterministic C_k, the pointwise bound |H_k Delta X_k|<=C_k(|X_k|+|X_{k-1}|) verifies the domain using monotonicity and linearity of the integral; this does not use the later preservation theorem. Changing finitely many representatives changes the finite sum only on a measurable null union. Outside this domain the same algebraic finite sum may exist but is not an integrable transform. For the almost-sure product bound, split off the measurable exceptional null set: its nonnegative integral is zero, so the pointwise estimate on its complement proves the same L1 bound.",
